    is a concatenation of producers:

      a single-table query   input 0's columns, then input 0's computed values
      a join or a chain      input 0, then input 1, … in the order declared
      a grouped result       the GROUP BY keys, then the aggregates

    A `ColumnRef` names one producer and an index inside it. The server turns
    that into the flat ordinal the kernel evaluates against, because only the
    server knows how wide each table is.

    ── Why not a flat ordinal, as the kernel itself uses ────────────────────────

    The kernel packs a joined row by table width and a client could do the same
    arithmetic. It would need every table's width, which this file deliberately
    does not publish; and the day a column is added to an earlier table, every
    reference past it points somewhere else, with no error anywhere — the "same
    query, different answer" failure this project treats as the worst kind.

    A fixed stride (input i's column c at i×1024+c) removes the width lookup and
    replaces it with a hard limit on table width that wraps silently past it.
    `ColumnRef` is that idea with an unbounded stride.

    Qualified names (`books.title`) were rejected twice over: this protocol
    addresses columns by ordinal precisely so a predicate needs no fallible name
    lookup, and a self-join has two inputs sharing one table name. An input is a
    position, which a self-join distinguishes and a name cannot.

    A separate expression type per shape — `JoinExpr`, `GroupExpr` — was
    rejected for the reason the kernel gives for not having one: it would be a
    second and third place for three-valued null semantics to be got wrong.

    ── What it buys beyond tidiness ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    Two refusals become decidable that a flat ordinal makes undecidable. A
    `HAVING` naming a column that is not grouped is a *kind* mismatch here, not
    an ordinal that happens to fall in range. And a computed value referenced
    from across a join — which the kernel's joined space has no slot for — is
    likewise a kind that cannot appear there, rather than an ordinal that lands
    on some other table's column.
